# Response headers for static assets.
#
# The build copies this file into dist/client/, where Workers static assets
# reads it. The `/*` rule applies to every asset response, 404.html included.
# It never reaches a response generated by Worker code: the contact endpoint
# applies the `/*` rule itself, through src/lib/response-headers.ts.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The CSP hashes are build output and they go stale.
#
# Astro emits two inline <script> blocks and one inline <style> on a page with
# an island, which today is `/` alone, for HeroField.vue: the `client:load`
# directive loader, the <astro-island> element that hydrates it, and
# `astro-island,astro-slot,astro-static-slot {display:contents}`. There is no
# `src` to point at and, on a static host, no request-time nonce to issue, so
# hashing is the only way to allow exactly those three. The JSON-LD block in
# BaseLayout.astro needs no hash: script-src gates scripts the browser runs,
# and a <script type="application/ld+json"> never runs. See
# src/lib/structured-data.ts.
#
# An Astro upgrade that changes one byte of that runtime changes its hash, the
# browser refuses the script, and the island never hydrates: the hero field on
# `/` stays blank and its pause control never appears. That failure is silent
# in the page and loud only in the console.
#
# tests/headers.spec.ts recomputes all three from dist/ and fails when this
# file and the build have drifted. Do not hand-edit a hash; re-run the suite,
# take the values from the failure message, and check the diff is only the
# Astro runtime. ARCHITECTURE.md > "The hashes go stale" has the rest.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `connect-src` names one other origin: Umami's collector.
#
# The tracker itself is served from this origin, so `script-src` stays `'self'`
# and it is only the data the tracker sends that leaves. `'self'` is restated
# because naming the directive stops it falling back to `default-src`.
# UMAMI_HOST_URL in src/lib/analytics.ts is the same value, and
# tests/headers.spec.ts fails when the two differ, since a mismatch refuses
# every event with nothing on the page to show it. /privacy discloses the
# host, so widening this means editing that page in the same commit.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HSTS is 180 days with includeSubDomains, and still without preload.
#
# It is the one header here that a browser keeps after the response is gone:
# for the whole max-age it refuses to reach this host over http, and a stored
# policy changes only when that browser revisits and receives a different
# value. includeSubDomains promises that every subdomain of sinduri.lol,
# present and future, is HTTPS-only, with no click-through, so give a new
# subdomain working TLS before anything is served on it. No preload: it bakes
# the host into browser binaries and getting back off takes months of release
# trains nobody here controls.
#
# Raised from max-age=86400 on 2026-09-11, to the value Cloudflare's zone HSTS
# setting was already serving in place of this header, so turning the zone
# setting off never weakens what browsers hold.
#
# tests/headers.spec.ts pins the max-age and asserts preload is absent, so
# neither can move by accident. scripts/check-live.sh compares what production
# sends with this file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A header may be set by one rule only.
#
# Cloudflare applies every rule below that matches a request, rather than
# letting the more specific one win, and joins two values for the same header
# name with a comma. For the headers here that is silent corruption:
# `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin, cross-origin` is not a policy any
# browser can parse, and a CSP joined to a second CSP is enforced as two
# policies at once, which is how a working policy becomes a blank page.
#
# So no header name appears under more than one pattern, with one exception,
# `Cache-Control`, which the two asset rules below detach with `! Cache-Control`
# before any value of their own. The asset worker applies matching rules in file
# order, each one deleting its detached names and then setting its own, so
# `/_astro/*` receives only its caching and `/vendor/*` no Cache-Control at
# all. Read from workers-shared's asset worker, which wrangler bundles:
# `attachCustomHeaders` in node_modules/miniflare/dist/src/workers/assets/.
# tests/headers.spec.ts asserts no other name is shared, and the exact value
# each kind of path receives.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why only /_astro/* is cached immutably.
#
# Vite content-hashes every filename it emits there, so the URL changes
# whenever the bytes do and a year-long immutable cache can never hold a stale
# one. Nothing else in the build has that property: the HTML lives at stable
# addresses, and public/ is copied through verbatim, so
# `/images/og-default.png` keeps its name across every edit.
#
# A browser holding one of these will not revalidate for a year and there is no
# way to reach in and clear it. Put an unhashed path under that rule and the
# file is frozen, for a year, in every browser that visited before the mistake.
#
# Everything else gets `no-transform` and nothing more, which replaces the
# platform's `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`; `/vendor/*` gets no
# Cache-Control at all. With no max-age and no Last-Modified a browser has
# nothing to compute freshness from, so it revalidates against the ETag, which
# is what a file at a stable URL wants.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `no-transform` is what keeps JavaScript Detections off every page.
#
# The Free plan cannot turn that feature off. It injects an inline script into
# every HTML response, with a per-request token no CSP hash can allow, so every
# page logged a CSP refusal, and it stripped the HTML's ETag. Cloudflare skips
# the injection when the response carries `Cache-Control: no-transform`.
# Measured 2026-09-13 with a Transform Rule on `/` alone: the script was gone
# from `/` in 3 of 3 requests, still on `/about/`, and the ETag came back.
# `npm run check:live` fails if the script returns.
#
# It also switches off Cloudflare's compression, which honours `no-transform`
# and which a Compression Rule cannot override. Measured 2026-09-13 on
# production: the stylesheet went out at 43 KB, the Vue runtime at 57 KB and
# the homepage at 31 KB, none of them encoded. The injection only ever touches
# HTML ("injected only in response to requests for HTML pages", in Cloudflare's
# JavaScript Detections docs), so the asset rules detach it to let their files
# be compressed. No measurement of this zone compressing them exists from
# before `no-transform`; `npm run check:live` is that measurement, and fails if
# an asset arrives unencoded. Cloudflare weakens a strong ETag when it
# compresses, and `If-None-Match` still answers 304 with a weak one. Pages stay
# uncompressed: HTML paths have no pattern that separates them from everything
# else, and a page without the injection is worth more than its bytes.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Link` names the three files an agent looks for before it reads a page:
# llms.txt as `describedby`, the sitemap, and the feed as an `alternate`. It is
# specification.website's "Link headers for discovery" item, and the syntax is
# RFC 8288. `describedby` and `alternate` are registered relations; `sitemap`
# is not, and is here because that checklist names it. Every page's <head>
# carries the same sitemap and feed links, for readers handed HTML.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Speculation-Rules` points at /speculationrules.json, which prefetches a
# same-origin page once a pointer rests on its link. A rules file named by this
# header is not subject to the CSP, where an inline
# `<script type="speculationrules">` would need a hash that goes stale on every
# edit. Prefetch, never prerender: a prerendered page runs the vendored Umami
# tracker, which does not check `document.prerendering`, and would count a
# visit nobody made. The file must be served as
# `application/speculationrules+json` or the browser ignores it, hence the rule
# for it below; `tests/served-types.spec.ts` checks the asset worker honours
# it. Chromium only, so it is an enhancement and nothing depends on it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The site is open to search indexes since 2026-09-14. It was closed by
# `X-Robots-Tag: noindex` here until launch. If it ever needs closing again,
# use that header rather than `Disallow` in robots.txt: a crawler told not to
# fetch a page never sees a noindex, and can still list the URL from a link.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-QzWFZi+FLIx23tnm9SBU4aEgx4x8DsuASP07mfqol/c=' 'sha256-Ya0pUYrC7nM5Cn/056TyVuEiz6dFGrzmkWzgON0pF0U='; style-src 'self' 'sha256-vv9IoKo7BSLbWcUHr3tNmfNVmm5L/9Cfn2H6LMk7/ow='; connect-src 'self' https://gateway.umami.is; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=15552000; includeSubDomains
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cache-Control: no-transform
  Speculation-Rules: "/speculationrules.json"
  Link: </llms.txt>; rel="describedby"; type="text/plain", </sitemap-index.xml>; rel="sitemap"; type="application/xml", </rss.xml>; rel="alternate"; type="application/rss+xml"

# Build output, and only build output. Every filename Vite emits here carries a
# content hash, which is what makes both of these safe. See the two notes
# above: why immutable is restricted to hashed names, and why neither header
# name may also appear under `/*`.
#
# No Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy, deliberately. It exists to unlock
# SharedArrayBuffer and precise timers, which nothing here uses, and its only
# other effect would be to break a cross-origin subresource on the day someone
# adds one.
#
# No Reporting-Endpoints either. It needs a collector to send reports to, and
# there is none; a header naming a dead endpoint is worse than no header.
/_astro/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin

# The vendored Umami tracker. Unhashed, so it is not cached immutably; it only
# loses `no-transform`, so that it is compressed. src/lib/analytics.ts says why
# it is vendored.
/vendor/*
  ! Cache-Control

# The speculation rules file. Its type decides whether a browser reads it at
# all; see the note above.
/speculationrules.json
  Content-Type: application/speculationrules+json
